---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/alternatives/firepush-alternative';
const title = "Firepush alternatives: hand over recovery without losing permission";
const desc = "Decide whether to retain web push, replace a legacy SMS or email path, or move browser recovery with a documented re-subscription plan.";
const faqs = [{"question": "Does leaving Firepush mean the company has shut down?", "answer": "No. Its current product is web push. An explicit November 2026 update says it no longer offers SMS or email campaigns; older help still describes those channels. Confirm legacy account access and records with support."}, {"question": "Can a subscriber CSV move browser delivery to another app?", "answer": "Do not assume that. Browser permission, service workers and provider tokens are separate from an audience export. Require the receiving provider to confirm a supported migration or plan fresh subscriptions."}, {"question": "Can web push permission authorize replacement SMS?", "answer": "No. Use a separate documented SMS permission record. An anonymous browser subscriber is not automatically an email address or a phone subscriber."}];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} dateModified="2026-11-25" />
  <article class="container py-16 prose-shopify">
    <a href="/alternatives">All alternatives</a>
    <h1>{title}</h1>
    <p>{desc}</p>
    <h2>Keep web push if that is the job you need</h2>
    <p>A focused browser recovery tool can remain useful beside a working email and SMS program. Repair stale cart links, excessive prompts or overlapping reminders before replacing a functioning push setup. For a hypothetical sneaker drop, retain a requested size-restock alert while suppressing a broad recovery offer after purchase. That is a proposed operating design, not a merchant case study. A move is worthwhile when the destination demonstrates a required recovery or reporting capability that the retained setup cannot provide.</p>
    <h2>Resolve today’s scope against legacy help</h2>
    <p>Checked November 25, 2026: the <a href="https://getfirepush.com/blog/targeted-email-marketing/">official article updated November 18, 2026</a> explicitly says Firepush is now a web push app and no longer offers SMS or email campaigns. Its <a href="https://getfirepush.com/help/settings-account/message-variables/">message-variable help</a> still describes SMS templates. Treat that help as legacy documentation, not a current entitlement. This is a channel change, not evidence of a company shutdown. Ask support which old records, exports and billing balances your account can still access before cancelling it.</p>
    <h2>Split the replacement decision by channel</h2>
    <p>Keeping Firepush push and moving only a legacy channel avoids an unnecessary browser rebuild. For SMS, investigate <a href="/apps/postscript">Postscript</a> against destination coverage, capture and service requirements. For email, evaluate your existing <a href="/apps/shopify-email">Shopify Email</a> setup before buying another layer. A broader <a href="/apps/omnisend">Omnisend</a> evaluation may suit a team seeking shared ownership; require a demonstration of each needed channel and current account scope. None of these choices implies automatic transfer of audiences, templates or history.</p>
    <h2>Secure legacy record access first</h2>
    <p>Before removing the app, inventory account administrators, report exports, historical campaign IDs, channel permission evidence, suppressions, template copy, discount codes and open support cases. Save the available export format and reconcile its record count with the account. Identify gaps explicitly rather than recreating consent retrospectively. Ask who retains access after cancellation and for how long; do not promise a retention period. Assign a records owner distinct from the operator who enables replacement sends.</p>
    <h2>Browser permission is not a portable address book</h2>
    <p>Firepush’s <a href="https://getfirepush.com/help/getting-started/switching-push-provider/">switching guide</a> describes provider-specific service workers and Firebase projects as a barrier to push subscriber transfer. That guide is about moving into Firepush; obtain explicit destination support for an outgoing migration instead of reversing its instructions. Inventory the store origin, worker registration, token ownership and supported browser paths. An exported identifier does not prove the new provider can deliver. Plan re-subscription where no supported transfer exists, with a conservative reachable-audience forecast.</p>
    <h2>Proposed prompt and token acceptance matrix</h2>
    <p>Use separate browsers with allowed, denied and default permission states, plus a previously subscribed browser whose permission was revoked. Check the supported mobile installation path too. Record which worker and token receive each controlled push after the new integration is enabled. The denied browser should not enter a repeated prompt loop; the default browser should see a deliberate invitation at a relevant moment. A returning subscriber may need a new subscription without changing the browser’s existing permission. Avoid deleting the old worker until the migration owner understands the effect on both paths.</p>
    <h2>Hand over cart recovery and expiring alerts</h2>
    <p>Choose one recovery owner for each cohort before turning on replacement automations. Queue a cart reminder, purchase before dispatch and inspect cancellation; repeat with a delayed purchase event and duplicate cart event. Change the variant, sell it out and expire a discount while an alert is waiting. The destination link and payload must still describe an available offer. A non-click is not proof of non-delivery and must not automatically trigger an SMS fallback. Only separately permitted shoppers can enter that fallback, under an explicit sequence and contact limit.</p>
    <h2>Keep service and marketing responsibility explicit</h2>
    <p>List each old order, shipment, refund, welcome and promotional template with its actual event source and current owner. Retain the working essential order-update system until the receiving team demonstrates it receives the right fulfillment event and provides a monitored support route. Browser delivery is not guaranteed, so an unreachable push audience must not silently lose essential service access. Apply channel withdrawals and broader customer requests according to their recorded scope; browser denial must never be treated as SMS consent or as an instruction to erase unrelated records.</p>
    <h2>Compare reachable recovery economics</h2>
    <p>Ask for current push send allowances and peak overage behavior separately from any replacement email contacts, SMS segments and carrier charges. Include re-subscription work, lost reachable browsers, overlap fees and support labor. In a proposed holdout exercise, assign eligible shoppers before recovery, measure net sales after discounts/refunds, then deduct actual goods, fulfillment, messaging and allocated platform/support costs. Keep the same observation period and denominator in each group. Hold fixed goods cost fixed when an offer reduces selling price; a cheaper send or attributed order count alone does not establish a profitable replacement.</p>
    <h2>Release, pause and recover the handover</h2>
    <p>Release one bounded recovery path after the permission matrix, purchase exit and expiry exercises meet criteria agreed by the operator. Record old and new queue counts, sends and exclusions. If duplicate alerts or stale links appear, pause the new dispatch first and reconcile the ledger before restoring eligible old queues. Preserve the old worker/account only as long as the supported handover requires; removal and token invalidation can limit rollback. Hand support the prompt behavior, record-access arrangements and unresolved subscribers. The <a href="/apps/firepush">existing app review</a> contains older SMS scope wording; use the explicit current vendor notice above for this channel decision.</p>
    <FaqSection faqs={faqs} />
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
